      "notes": "All five claims are well-supported by their cited evidence. The stated-tier claims quote the policy text verbatim. E5 (IFS) directly entails the measurable claim about energy-efficiency equalisation. E21 (IFS) supports the projected claim about distributional effects depending on funding \u2014 the tier is correctly marked projected. E26 supports the measurable claim about existing ECO4/GBIS targeting. Tier integrity holds: no projection is dressed as measurable. Magnitude (minor) and time_horizon (this-parliament) are defensible given the IFS baseline and unspecified funding. The biggest_unknown about levy-based funding is a genuine crux, not a lazy hedge. No party identity or motive-attribution appears in the rationale. The 'improves' direction is supported by the progressive design claim with the IFS counter-constraint properly incorporated as a limiting factor rather than a false-balance move \u2014 the net lean is evidence-justified. No lever leakage: the verdict judges distributional effect on inequality, not energy policy for its own sake."
